#Diary In Code
#### Short answer

**Don’t wait until you finish all the physics before learning to program.**  
Python (and Jupyter) are *tools* that will make every physics concept you learn easier to explore, verify, and remember. You can learn the basics of programming **in parallel** with the early math/physics courses, and you’ll feel more confident when you hit the more advanced topics.

---

## Why Programming Helps (and How It Fits Into Your Plan)

| What you’ll learn first | How it supports physics |
|--------------------------|-------------------------|
| **Python basics** (variables, loops, functions, lists, arrays) | Allows you to write quick scripts to check algebraic manipulations or generate data for graphs. |
| **Jupyter notebooks** | Keeps code, equations, and notes together. Great for debugging and presenting solutions. |
| **NumPy / SciPy** | Handles large arrays, linear‑algebra operations, and differential‑equation solvers—core to many physics problems. |
| **Matplotlib** | Visualize vectors, fields, trajectories, probability distributions—all essential for learning intuition. |

> **Bottom line:** *Programming is a skill, not a prerequisite.*  
> You can read a physics textbook, solve the problem on paper, then **test** your solution with a tiny Python script. Doing this repeatedly turns abstract concepts into concrete, observable patterns.

---

## Suggested Learning Order (Hybrid)

| Phase | What to do | Why |
|-------|------------|-----|
| **0–1 month** | • Install Anaconda (Python + Jupyter). <br>• Complete a 1‑week “Python for beginners” tutorial (e.g., *Python Crash Course* or *Codecademy*). | Get comfortable with syntax, loops, and basic data structures. |
| **1–3 months** | • Start the **Calculus 1** course (MIT OCW 18.01). <br>• In parallel, practice a few Python exercises that mimic calculus operations (derivatives, integrals, plotting functions). | Builds math foundation while reinforcing programming as a “toolkit.” |
| **3–6 months** | • Continue with **Linear Algebra** (MIT OCW 18.06). <br>• Write Python scripts that compute eigenvalues, solve linear systems, and visualize eigenvectors. | Physics heavily relies on linear algebra; coding these concepts solidifies understanding. |
| **6–12 months** | • Begin **Classical Mechanics** (MIT OCW 8.01). <br>• Use Python to simulate simple systems (harmonic oscillator, pendulum, two‑body problem). | Physics becomes tangible when you see it “run” on the computer. |
| **12–18 months** | • Dive into **Electromagnetism** and **Statistical Mechanics**. <br>• Write more advanced simulations (e.g., Maxwell’s equations in 1‑D, Monte‑Carlo for Ising model). | At this point, you’re comfortable with both the physics and the code. |

> **Tip:** Keep a *shared notebook* (GitHub repo or local folder) for each major topic. Include the textbook notes, code, plots, and a short summary. You’ll have a living portfolio that proves your progress and is great for grad‑school applications.

